{"text": "OFFICE OF THE SECRETARY OF DEFENSE\n1000 DEFENSE PENTAGON\nWASHINGTON, D.C. 20301-1000\n\n2,/\u00a2/8\n\nMEMORANDUM FOR CHAIRMAN OF THE JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF\nSUBJECT: Planning and Execution Guidance for a Veteran\u2019s Day Parade\n\nThe purpose of this memorandum is to provide initial guidance for the planning and\nexecution of a military-themed Veteran\u2019s Day Parade in Washington, D.C. on November 11,\n2018. This parade will focus on the contributions of our Veterans throughout the history of the\nU.S. Military, starting from the Revolutionary War and the War of 1812 to today, with an\nemphasis on the price of freedom. The Joint Staff will serve as the Office of Primary\nResponsibility for planning this parade while NORTHCOM will lead the execution. The\nfollowing is a list of planning considerations provided by the Secretary of Defense:\n\nThe parade will integrate with the annual D.C. Veteran\u2019s Day Parade.\nThe parade route will be from the White House to the Capitol.\nInclude the Medal of Honor Association and other Veterans Service Organizations.\nVeterans and Medal of Honor recipients should be surrounding POTUS in the\nreviewing area at the Capitol.\ne The parade will highlight the evolution of women Veterans from separate formations\nin World War II to today\u2019s integrated formations.\n\u00a9 Represent Veterans from previous wars by formations wearing period uniforms.\ne Utilize the Old Guard Fife and Drum, uniforms used at Anniversary celebrations, and\nreenactments.\ne Include wheeled vehicles only, no tanks \u2014 consideration must be given to minimize\ndamage to local infrastructure.\n* Include a heavy air component at the end of the parade, to include older aircraft as\navailable.\n\nHallock N. hier,\nCAPT, USN\n\nExecutive Secretary\n\n1 URUA\n\n1029 14-18/CMD003678:18",
